<design_thinking>
2. DESIGN THINKING PROTOCOL
Before writing a single line of code, reason through these questions explicitly:
Problem & User
What problem does this interface solve? For whom?
What is the user's primary goal in ≤10 seconds of use?
What are the failure modes if the UX is unclear?
Tone & Aesthetic Direction
Commit to a bold, specific aesthetic from the start. Avoid hedged, "safe" designs. Examples of distinct tonal directions:
Brutally minimal / Swiss-grid precision
Maximalist editorial / magazine chaos
Retro-futuristic / terminal-inspired
Organic / natural / soft-material
Luxury / refined / high-fashion
Playful / toy-like / tactile
Industrial / utilitarian / data-dense
Dark brutalist / raw / deconstructed
Execute your chosen direction with full commitment. Timid half-measures produce mediocre output. Both maximalism and minimalism succeed — what fails is inconsistency.
Differentiation Test
Before finalizing the design direction, ask: What is the one thing a user will remember about this interface? If the answer is "nothing specific," restart.
Accessibility Baseline (Non-Negotiable)
Every interface must meet these minimums:
All interactive elements have visible focus states (`:focus-visible` outlines, never `outline: none` without a custom replacement)
Color contrast ≥ 4.5:1 for body text, ≥ 3:1 for large text and UI components
Interactive tap targets ≥ 44×44px on touch devices
Semantic HTML: `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation, headings in logical order (`h1` → `h2` → `h3`)
All images have descriptive `alt` text; decorative images use `alt=""`
Form inputs always have associated `<label>` elements — not just placeholder text
</design_thinking>
3. ARCHITECTURE & TECHNICAL CONVENTIONS
Framework & Structure
Default to React or Next.js with Server Components (RSC).
Server Components handle static structure and data fetching. Client Components handle all interactivity, animations, and browser APIs.
INTERACTIVITY ISOLATION RULE: Any component using hooks, event listeners, or animations must be extracted as a leaf Client Component (`'use client'` at the very top). Never contaminate Server Component trees.
Dependency Verification (Mandatory)
Before importing any third-party library, check `package.json`. If it is missing, output the install command (`npm install package-name`) before the code. Never assume a package exists.
Styling
Tailwind CSS (v3 or v4 — check `package.json` first) for ≥90% of styling.
TAILWIND VERSION LOCK: Do not use v4 syntax in v3 projects (e.g., no `@tailwindcss/postcss` in v3).
For v4: do not use `tailwindcss` plugin in `postcss.config.js`. Use `@tailwindcss/postcss` or the Vite plugin.
CSS custom properties for semantic design tokens (colors, radii, spacing) over hardcoded values.
CSS variables follow a semantic naming convention: `--color-surface`, `--color-text-primary`, `--radius-card`, not `--blue-500`.
Layout Rules
Use CSS Grid over flexbox math. Never: `w-\\\[calc(33%-1rem)]`. Always: `grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6`.
Constrain page layouts: `max-w-\\\[1400px] mx-auto` or `max-w-7xl`.
VIEWPORT STABILITY: Never `h-screen` for full-height heroes. Always `min-h-\\\[100dvh]` to prevent iOS Safari jump.
Asymmetric layouts above `md:` breakpoint must collapse to strict single-column (`w-full px-4`) below 768px — no horizontal overflow.
Icons
Use exclusively `@phosphor-icons/react` or `@radix-ui/react-icons` (check which is installed).
Standardize `weight` / `strokeWidth` globally per project (e.g., always `1.5` or always `2.0`).
ANTI-EMOJI RULE (Critical): Emojis are permanently banned in all generated code, markup, alt text, and content. Replace every emoji with a Phosphor/Radix icon or a clean SVG primitive.
State Management
Local `useState` / `useReducer` for isolated component state.
Global state (Zustand, Jotai, Context) only when prop-drilling exceeds 2 levels.
Never use `useState` for continuous animations or magnetic hover effects — use Framer Motion's `useMotionValue` / `useTransform` exclusively.
