4. UX PATTERNS & INTERACTION DESIGN
This section defines precise interaction design rules. These govern how an interface behaves — not just how it looks.
4.1 Information Architecture
Progressive Disclosure
Surface only what the user needs at each step. Reveal complexity on demand:
Primary actions visible immediately; secondary actions behind disclosure (dropdown, accordion, "More options")
Destructive or irreversible actions always require an explicit confirmation step (dialog, inline confirm, or type-to-confirm)
Never display more than 7 ± 2 items in a navigation menu, dropdown, or list without grouping or search
Visual Hierarchy (F-Pattern & Z-Pattern Awareness)
Hero sections: most important information top-left for left-to-right scanning
Use size, weight, and color contrast (not just size alone) to establish hierarchy
A page should have exactly one primary CTA. Supporting actions are secondary (lower contrast, smaller, outlined)
Avoid "hierarchy collapse": when everything is bold, nothing is
Proximity & Grouping (Gestalt)
Related controls must share a visual container, spacing unit, or divider
Unrelated controls need clear separation (whitespace, border, or background shift)
Form fields related to the same concept should be grouped with a shared label group, not scattered
4.2 Navigation Patterns
Primary Navigation
Highlight the current route with both a visual indicator (background, underline, or active dot) and `aria-current="page"` on the active link
Mobile nav: hamburger menus must have an animated open/close icon transition and trap focus within the drawer when open
Sticky headers must not exceed 64px height on mobile; use `backdrop-blur` + semi-transparent background for layered depth
Breadcrumbs
Required for pages 3+ levels deep
Use `>` or `/` separator, not `→` (arrow implies action/navigation forward, not hierarchy)
Last breadcrumb item represents current page and is non-interactive
Pagination vs. Infinite Scroll
Use pagination when users need to return to a specific position (search results, tables, data lists)
Use infinite scroll only for content-consumption contexts (social feeds, image galleries)
Infinite scroll must include a "Back to top" affordance after 3+ loads
4.3 Form Design (High-Precision Rules)
Forms are the highest-stakes UX surface. Every detail matters.
Label Positioning
Labels sit above inputs, always. Placeholder-only labeling is an accessibility failure
Use `for`/`id` pairing on every `<label>` / `<input>` — not wrapping label as a workaround
Floating labels (animated from inside the input) are acceptable only when space is critically constrained
Input Anatomy
```
\\\[Label text]              ← Always above, 12px gap below
\\\[Input field]             ← 44px min height
\\\[Helper text]             ← Optional, subdued, 4px gap below input
\\\[Error message]           ← Red, with error icon, replaces helper text on error
```
Validation Timing
Never validate on every keystroke for format errors (email, phone) — validate `onBlur` or on submit
Exception: Character count limits, password strength meters, and "username available" checks may validate in real time since they provide progressive feedback
On submit, scroll to and focus the first invalid field
Error messages must name the problem specifically: "Email must include '@'" not "Invalid input"
Input States (All Must Be Implemented)
Every input must have distinct visual treatment for:
Default (resting)
Focused (ring/border highlight, `focus-visible` only — not `:focus`)
Filled (user has entered a value)
Error (red border + error message + icon)
Disabled (reduced opacity, `cursor-not-allowed`, `aria-disabled`)
Read-only (visually distinct from disabled; user can still copy text)
Loading/pending (inline spinner, field not editable)
Multi-Step Forms
Show a progress indicator (step dots, step counter "Step 2 of 4", or linear bar)
Persist data across steps — never lose a user's input on "Back"
Validate only the current step before advancing, not the entire form
Buttons in Forms
Primary submit button is right-aligned (Western reading convention for form completion)
"Cancel" or "Back" is left-aligned or text-only (lower visual weight)
Disable submit button while submission is in progress; show inline loading state on the button itself
4.4 Feedback & System Status
Following Nielsen's heuristic: the system must always keep users informed about what is going on.
Loading States
Skeleton loaders must match the exact layout shape of the content they replace (never generic spinners for content areas)
Skeleton shimmer direction: left-to-right, 1.5s duration, `animation-timing-function: ease-in-out`
Full-page loading: a thin progress bar at the top edge (e.g., NProgress pattern), not a centered spinner
Button loading state: replace label with inline spinner + "Loading…" text; maintain button width to prevent layout shift
Empty States
Every list, table, or data view must have a designed empty state:
```
\\\[Illustrative icon or minimal SVG illustration]
\\\[Heading: what is empty]
\\\[Subtext: why it might be empty]
\\\[Optional CTA: primary action to populate this view]
```
Empty states are not error states. Tone should be helpful and inviting, not apologetic.
Success States
Inline success: green checkmark replacing the submit button for 2–3 seconds before resetting or redirecting
Toast notifications: top-right positioning, auto-dismiss after 4s, manually dismissible, maximum 1 stacked at a time for actions (not for system alerts)
Toast must include an action link when the success has a next step ("View order →")
Error States
Distinguish between user errors (correctable, inline, non-destructive) and system errors (full error state, retry affordance)
Network errors: show a retry button + estimated retry delay, never just "Something went wrong"
404 pages: offer navigation back home + search; never a dead end
Confirmation Dialogs
Use for destructive actions only (delete, remove, cancel subscription)
Dialog must name the specific item being deleted: "Delete 'Q4 Report.pdf'?" not "Delete this file?"
"Cancel" is always the default-focused button (safe exit), not the destructive action
Destructive button is never the primary/filled style — use a red outlined or ghost variant
4.5 Interactive Affordances & Micro-Interactions
Hover States
Every interactive element must have a distinct hover state (background shift, color shift, underline, shadow change)
Hover state transitions: `150ms ease-out` for entry, `100ms ease-in` for exit (faster exit feels snappier)
Hover states must not shift adjacent content (no layout changes on hover)
Active / Pressed States
On `:active`, apply physical press simulation: `scale(0.97)` or `translateY(1px)` — never both simultaneously
Active transition: `75ms ease-in` (near-instant, simulates physical resistance)
For toggle buttons (active/inactive), use a filled background for active state and a subtle border for inactive
Focus Management
After closing a modal or popover, focus must return to the trigger element
After navigating to a new page/route, focus moves to the `<h1>` or main content region (skip links for keyboard users)
Tab order must follow visual reading order — never create focus traps outside modals
Tactile Feedback Checklist
Before marking any interactive component complete, verify:
[ ] Hover state defined
[ ] Active/pressed state defined
[ ] Focus-visible state defined (ring, not just color shift)
[ ] Disabled state defined (when applicable)
[ ] Loading/pending state defined (when applicable)
[ ] Keyboard navigation works without a mouse
