6. MOTION & ANIMATION
6.1 Motion Principles
Purpose Before Performance
Every animation must serve one of these functional goals:
Orientation: show where an element came from or went (transition direction matches spatial relationship)
Feedback: confirm a user action occurred
Continuity: maintain context during state changes (shared element transitions)
Delight: enrich the experience without slowing the user down
If an animation serves none of these, remove it.
Timing Guidelines
```
Micro-interactions (hover, press):    50–150ms
Simple transitions (show/hide):       150–250ms
Complex reveals (page sections):      300–500ms
Choreographed sequences:              600–1200ms total, never longer
```
Easing
Never use linear easing for anything visible to users
Default: `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` (fast out, slight overshoot) — "snappy spring"
Exits: `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)` (ease-in) — elements accelerate out of view
Spring physics (Framer): `type: "spring", stiffness: 100, damping: 20` for interactive elements
Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: wrap all non-essential animations:
```css
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    \\\*, \\\*::before, \\\*::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; }
  }
  ```
6.2 Motion by MOTION_INTENSITY Dial
Level 1–3 (Static)
CSS `:hover` and `:active` states only
`transition: opacity 150ms, transform 150ms` for show/hide
No automatic animations, no loops, no entrance sequences
Level 4–7 (Fluid CSS)
Entrance animations on mount: `opacity 0→1` + `translateY(8px→0)` with `animation-delay` staggering
`transition: all 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` on interactive elements
`will-change: transform` used sparingly (only on elements that will definitely animate)
Focus exclusively on `transform` and `opacity` — never animate `top`, `left`, `width`, `height`
Level 8–10 (Advanced Choreography)
Framer Motion for all interactive components
`layout` and `layoutId` props for smooth reordering and shared element transitions
Staggered children: `staggerChildren: 0.06` in parent variants; child variants with `hidden/visible` states
Magnetic cursor effects using `useMotionValue` / `useTransform` outside the render cycle
Perpetual micro-animations (pulse, float, shimmer, typewriter) isolated in `React.memo` Client Components
`AnimatePresence` around any conditionally rendered content
Never use `window.addEventListener('scroll')` — use Framer's `useScroll` or Intersection Observer
CRITICAL Performance Rules
Perpetual/infinite animations: isolated in their own microscopic `React.memo` Client Component
Grain/noise textures: `fixed` + `pointer-events-none` pseudo-element only — never on scrolling containers
Z-index: only for systemic layer contexts (navbars: 40, modals: 50, tooltips: 60) — never arbitrary
GPU acceleration: `transform: translateZ(0)` or `will-change: transform` on animated elements
No Framer Motion + GSAP in the same component tree; if GSAP is used for scroll-storytelling, it lives in its own isolated `useEffect` with strict cleanup
