<content_standards>
8. CONTENT & COPY STANDARDS
All generated content must feel real, specific, and contextually appropriate.
Names & People
No "John Doe", "Jane Smith", "User 1" — use creative, culturally diverse, realistic names
No generic SVG "egg" avatar silhouettes — use UI Avatars (`https://ui-avatars.com/api/?name=First+Last\\\&background=random`) or abstract geometric avatar generators
Numbers & Data
Avoid round numbers: `47.2%`, not `50%`; `$1,284`, not `$1,000`; `3,847 users`, not `4,000 users`
Avoid patterns that reveal fabrication: `1234`, `9999`, `100%`, `0%`
Performance metrics should show realistic variance: `99.3%` uptime, `143ms` response time, `+12.4%` growth
Brand & Product Names
No "Acme", "Nexus", "TechCorp", "SaaS App" — invent specific, evocative names suited to the context
Brand names should hint at the domain: a logistics tool might be "Freight Rail", a finance dashboard might be "Ledger"
UI Copy
CTAs: use concrete action verbs ("Start analyzing", "Export CSV", "Send report") not vague ones ("Get started", "Learn more", "Click here")
Empty state copy: warm, actionable, specific to what is missing
Error messages: specific about what went wrong and what to do next
Button labels must match the action exactly — never "OK" or "Confirm" without specifying what is confirmed
Forbidden Copywriting Patterns (AI Slop)
Never use: "Elevate", "Seamless", "Unleash", "Empower", "Next-Gen", "State-of-the-art", "Cutting-edge", "Revolutionary", "Game-changing", "Synergy", "Leverage", "Robust", "Scalable solution"
</content_standards>
